  <!-- BACKGROUND -->
  <ShowcaseSection label="Background" heading="A Real Business, Old-School Tools">
    <p>Two people run the whole operation, no technical background, mostly from their phones.</p>
    <p>Every operation lived in one spreadsheet: 21 sheets, one per month per product. Each row was a delivery — date, delivery-note number, liters, town, customer name, invoice number, and a free-text payment status. Alongside it: cloud accounting software (Morning / Green Invoice) that didn't talk to the spreadsheet at all. Every match between a delivery note, an invoice, and a receipt happened by hand, from memory.</p>
    <p>The exact pains the business described:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>"Who still hasn't paid?" — no answer without going sheet by sheet.</li>
      <li>Post-dated checks, sometimes six months out, noted in a free-text comment and forgotten.</li>
      <li>No inventory picture: how much came in from the supplier versus how much went out.</li>
      <li>Logging a delivery from the field meant waiting for the evening and a computer.</li>
    </ul>
  </ShowcaseSection>

  <!-- THE RESEARCH -->
  <ShowcaseSection label="The Research" heading="What Was Actually in the File">
    <p>Before a single line of code: a deep, script-based analysis of the file. The file itself was never opened "by hand" — it was too big and too fragile for that. The findings dictated the entire architecture.</p>
    <p>There was no master data at all. No customer list, no supplier list, no invoice table. Every business entity existed only as free text inside delivery rows. The schema had to be derived, not copied.</p>
    <p>Real scope: ~1,900 delivery rows total — the volume of records the investigation into how the business actually operated had to untangle and match, not an annual rate. Diesel — few large deliveries (commercial, hundreds to thousands of liters); heating oil — many small deliveries (retail heating), only showing up October through March. Two product lines with opposite characters, in the same file.</p>
    <p>Data quality — a real minefield:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Dates in 4 different formats, including malformed entries like <code>26/11*2025</code> and <code>16/092025</code>, plus outright wrong years (<code>2925</code>, <code>3036</code>); the header row sat on a different line number in every sheet.</li>
      <li>~916 different spellings for customer names — the same customer appearing in 3+ variants.</li>
      <li>The "invoice" column held a real number in newer sheets and the word "issued" (a status word, not a number) in older ones.</li>
      <li>Multi-value cells ("697+698" — two delivery notes crammed into one cell), and two separate "quantity" columns in the same sheet (in vs. out).</li>
      <li>The "paid" column mixed status, payment method, installment plan, and reminders ("pick up check 05.03") — all in a single cell.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A surprising finding worth its own feature: tucked in the corner of every sheet was a free-text note about that month's purchase-price change ("up 22 agorot," "down 46 agorot"). A genuinely critical business figure, living as a margin note. In the new system, it became its own table with a dedicated screen.</p>
  </ShowcaseSection>

  <!-- HARDEST PROBLEM -->
  <ShowcaseSection label="The Hardest Problem" heading="Deduplicating Hebrew Names" accent="gold">
    <p>Merging ~916 name variants down to a few hundred real customers, in Hebrew, without steamrolling anyone:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Round 1 — normalization: stripped extra spaces, spacing around hyphens, stray quote marks.</li>
      <li>Round 2 — spacing-independent similarity: unified hyphenated and compound-word variants.</li>
      <li>Round 3 — Levenshtein distance + word order + clustering: caught misspellings and word-order swaps. 40 merges came out of this round alone.</li>
      <li>A human stayed in the loop: every round produced a CSV of proposed merges — nothing merged automatically without sign-off. Rejected merges were remembered, so they wouldn't be proposed a second time.</li>
      <li>An aliases table: every historical spelling is kept and mapped to its canonical customer, so search and tagging work on the old spelling too. Today: ~1,400 aliases.</li>
    </ol>
  </ShowcaseSection>

  <!-- THE MORNING INTEGRATION -->
  <ShowcaseSection label="Integrations" heading="The Morning Integration">
    <p>The official API documentation is a single-page app that doesn't load with a plain fetch request — pulling out the actual reference meant reverse-engineering it from the page's own JavaScript. The output was a full mapping document of every relevant endpoint, kept in the project so future work never has to repeat that.</p>
    <p>The sync itself: a JWT token, then pulling documents (invoices, receipts, credits), then an upsert keyed on the Morning document ID, then linking those documents to customers and balances. Reconciling the historical backlog used a script that verified counts against Morning directly. Delivery-note-to-invoice-to-receipt relationships are modeled many-to-many, because in the real data one invoice can cover several delivery notes, and one delivery note can be split across invoices.</p>
  </ShowcaseSection>

  <!-- WORKING WITH AI AGENTS -->
  <ShowcaseSection label="Process" heading="Working With AI Agents — What It Looked Like in Practice">
    <p>The project was built developer-led, with AI agents doing the work, on a few principles that held up:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Hard rules in guidance files: never open the spreadsheet in the main working context (it burns context budget), never run a heavy process without approval (the machine is load-sensitive), no pill shapes in the design. A rule written once got enforced in every session after.</li>
      <li>Internal skills for repeated procedures: how to run a migration, how to verify results without overloading the machine, Morning-specific conventions — written once as reusable skills, loaded by any agent when needed.</li>
      <li>Memory across sessions: decisions and constraints are kept in memory files. Even when a session got wiped mid-project (it happened), the context survived.</li>
      <li>A human approves, an agent executes: customer merges, data deletions, and deployments always went through a human sign-off at the decision point.</li>
    </ul>
  </ShowcaseSection>

